.th IPATCH VIII May/76
.sh NAME
ipatch \*- first aid for sick file systems
.sh SYNOPSIS
.bd /etc/ipatch
device
[ inumber ]
.sh DESCRIPTION
Ipatch is an interactive programme for patching broken inodes on filesystems.
When you invoke it, you must give it two arguments, the name of the
filesystem you would like to patch, and the inumber of the inode you are
interested in.
Ipatch will prompt you for commands by typing a dot `.'.
All commands work on a copy
of the inode which ipatch keeps in core.
No change is made to the file system being patched unless the user
explicitly writes out the inode using the w command.
Each command consists of a single letter, possibly followed by one or two
numeric arguments,
and perhaps terminated with the letter `p',
in which case the inode being editted is printed after the command is
executed.
Every command must be typed on a single line.
Whenever a number is called for, if the
first digit is a `0', the number is taken to be octal. Otherwise,
the number is taken to be decimal, just as in C.
If you type an erroneous command, ipatch
will ring the bell, type a question mark, and prompt you for another command.
Typing del (rubout) will cause ipatch to stop what it is doing and
prompt you for another command.
.ti+5
In the following description of the commands recognized by ipatch,
`i' and `j' uniformly refer to two numbers which are arguments of
the command being described.
.s3
.lp +9 8
.s3
a i j	addr[i] in the inode is set to j.
.lp +9 8
.s3
e i	If i is a valid inumber, the corresponding inode is
read in to be editted.
.lp +9 8
.s3
f i	The flag word is set to i.
.lp +9 8
.s3
g i	The groupid byte is set to i.
.lp +9 8
.s3
l i	The link count is set to i.
.lp +9 8
.s3
p	The contents of the inode are printed out.
Numbers in parentheses are in octal, numbers not in parentheses are in
decimal.
.lp +9 8
.s3
q	Exit from ipatch.
End of file on standard input (ctrl-d from a terminal) also causes
ipatch to exit.
.lp +9 8
.s3
s i j	The high order byte of the size is set to i,
and the low order word is set to j.
.lp +9 8
.s3
u i	The userid byte is set to i.
.lp +9 8
.s3
w	The incore copy of the inode is written out to the disk.
.lp +9 8
.s3
!	The rest of the line following the exclamation point is interpreted
as a UNIX command line by the shell.
.i0
.dt
.sh "SEE ALSO"
fs (V), [din]check (VIII), check (VIII), clri (VIII)
.sh AUTHOR
Tom Duff.
.sh BUGS
It believes the isize in the superblock of the filesystem.
.br
You can't patch indirect blocks.
.br
It's not clairvoyant.
